{
  "rules": [
    "Systematic Underestimation of Bullish Spikes: The model consistently fails to capture the magnitude of upward price surges (e.g., the rise from ~67 to ~80), forecasting only a flat or slightly upward trend. Apply a positive bias adjustment of +10 to +15 EUR when the 3-day historical momentum is strongly positive.",
    "Excessive Lag in Trend Reversal: Forecasts exhibit a significant delay in reacting to trend changes, often continuing to rise after the peak or falling after the trough. Implement a lag-correction factor that accelerates the forecast slope by 2x during the first 5 days of a confirmed trend shift.",
    "Failure to Anticipate Mean Reversion: After reaching peak levels (~80 EUR), the model continues to project gradual increases or stability, missing the sharp subsequent decline. Trigger a mean-reversion heuristic that forces a downward slope adjustment immediately upon detecting a price peak exceeding the 20-day moving average by more than 10%.",
    "Sentiment Ignorance: The model ignores sentiment spikes (values > 0.20) which historically precede the major price rallies. Integrate a sentiment multiplier: if sentiment > 0.20 in the last 3 days, increase the forecast slope by 0.5 EUR/day for the next 7 days.",
    "Horizon-Specific Overshoot: The model's long-term forecast (days 15-30) is too optimistic relative to the actual volatility, which tends to settle lower after the initial spike. Apply a dampening factor of 0.8 to the forecast values for days 10 through 30 to account for the rapid post-spike decay."
  ]
}
